# Process definitions for honcho, which runs the API and the Celery worker side by side in a
# single container. See ADR-0008 for why they are colocated rather than deployed separately:
# Render's free tier does not offer Background Workers.
#
# honcho forwards SIGTERM to both children and exits when either one dies, so a crashed worker
# takes the container down and gets restarted by the platform — rather than leaving a healthy
# API quietly accepting uploads that nothing will ever process.

web: /app/.venv/bin/uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port ${PORT:-8000} --proxy-headers --forwarded-allow-ips=*

# --concurrency=1: one child process. The free instance has 512 MB shared with the API, and
#   each additional child is a full Python interpreter.
# --max-tasks-per-child=1: recycle the child after EVERY task. ADR-0009 measured the embedding
#   model at ~200 MB resident, and process exit is what actually returns it to the OS -- CPython
#   does not reliably hand freed arenas back, and ONNX Runtime allocates natively. A higher
#   value keeps that 200 MB resident alongside the API on a 512 MB instance.
# -B: embedded celery beat — the scheduler that fires the maintenance sweepers. Fine with a
#   single worker instance; must become its own process if workers ever scale out.
# --without-gossip/--without-mingle: inter-worker coordination chatter that only matters in a
#   multi-worker cluster. With one worker it is pure Redis traffic and slower startup.
worker: /app/.venv/bin/celery -A app.workers.celery_app.celery_app worker --loglevel=info --concurrency=1 --max-tasks-per-child=1 --without-gossip --without-mingle -B
